ging elements with lst2.\n    The function counts odd numbers in lst1 and even numbers in lst2 to determine feasibility.\"\"\"\n    odd_count_lst1 = 0\n    for num in lst1:\n        if num % 2 != 0:\n            odd_count_lst1 += 1\n\n    even_count_lst2 = 0\n    for num in lst2:\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            even_count_lst2 += 1\n\n    if odd_count_lst1 <= even_count_lst2:\n        return \"YES\"\n    else:\n        return \"NO\"", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/118": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/118", "solution": "def get_closest_vowel(word):\n    \"\"\"Finds the closest vowel from the right side of a word that is between two consonants.\n    Returns the vowel character or an empty string if no such vowel is found.\n    \"\"\"\n    vowels = \"aeiouAEIOU\"\n\n    def _is_vowel(char):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a character is a vowel (case-sensitive).\"\"\"\n        return char in vowels\n\n    def _is_consonant(char):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a character is an English letter and not a vowel.\"\"\"\n        return 'a' <= char <= 'z' and char not in vowels or \\\n               'A' <= char <= 'Z' and char not in vowels\n\n    # Iterate from the second-to-last character down to the second character (index 1).\n    # This excludes the first and last characters from being the vowel itself,\n    # and ensures there are always prev and next characters.\n    for i in range(len(word) - 2, 0, -1):\n        current_char = word[i]\n        prev_char = word[i-1]\n        next_char = word[i+1]\n\n        if _is_vowel(current_char) and _is_consonant(prev_char) and _is_consonant(next_char):\n            return current_char\n\n    return \"\"", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/117": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/117", "solution": "def select_words(s, n):\n    \"\"\"Given a string s and a natural number n, you have been tasked to implement \n    a function that returns a list of all words from string s that contain exactly \n    n consonants, in order these words appear in the string s.\n    If the string s is empty then the function should return an empty list.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    def _is_vowel(char):\n        \"\"\"Determines if a given character is a vowel (case-insensitive).\"\"\"\n        return char.lower() in {'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'}\n\n    def _count_consonants(word):\n        \"\"\"Counts the number of consonants in a given word.\"\"\"\n        consonant_count = 0\n        for char in word:\n            if char.isalpha() and not _is_vowel(char):\n                consonant_count += 1\n        return consonant_count\n\n    def _split_string_into_words(s_input):\n        \"\"\"Splits an input string into a list of individual words, handling multiple spaces.\"\"\"\n        return s_input.split()\n\n    if not s:\n        return []\n\n    words = _split_string_into_words(s)\n    result_list = []\n    for word in words:\n        if _count_consonants(word) == n:\n            result_list.append(word)\n    return result_list", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/116": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/116", "solution": "def sort_array(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    In this Kata, you have to sort an array of non-negative integers according to\n    number of ones in their binary representation in ascending order.\n    For similar number of ones, sort based on decimal value.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _count_ones(n):\n        # Counts the number of set bits (ones) in the binary representation of a non-negative integer.\n        return bin(n).count('1')\n\n    # The sorting key is a tuple: (number of ones, decimal value).\n    # Python's default sorting for tuples applies lexicographical order.\n    return sorted(arr, key=lambda x: (_count_ones(x), x))", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/12